1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,867
On the eighth of November 2026, the board of Nvidia, the world's most important AI chip company,

2
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:11,296
opened an independent investigation into its own founder, Jensen Huang.

3
00:00:11,296 --> 00:00:17,810
The reason: reports that he was the target of a Chinese intelligence effort, a so-called honey trap.

4
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,460
In this special, we'll answer four questions.

5
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:23,858
Who is Jensen Huang?

6
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:26,585
What is a GPU, and why does AI need one?

7
00:00:26,585 --> 00:00:31,243
Why are memory makers like SK Hynix and Micron suddenly so important?

8
00:00:31,243 --> 00:00:36,133
And what could this scandal mean for the AI buildout, and for India?

9
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:39,223
Let's start with the man.

10
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:42,666
Jensen Huang was born in Tainan, Taiwan, in 1963.

11
00:00:42,666 --> 00:00:49,894
On The Joe Rogan Experience, released on the third of December 2025, he told much of this story himself.

12
00:00:49,894 --> 00:00:57,605
One: in 1973, aged nine, he and his older brother were sent to America, to a boarding school in rural Kentucky.

13
00:00:57,605 --> 00:01:02,517
He was the youngest pupil, and his job was cleaning the bathrooms.

14
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:10,603
His parents followed about two years later, with very little money.

15
00:01:10,603 --> 00:01:18,627
Two: he worked his way up, studied electrical engineering at Oregon State University, and designed chips at AMD and LSI Logic.

16
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:28,277
And three: in April 1993, with two friends, Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, he started Nvidia.

17
00:01:28,277 --> 00:01:34,242
The plan was sketched in a Denny's diner booth: build chips for 3D video games.

18
00:01:37,133 --> 00:01:38,765
So what exactly is a GPU?

19
00:01:38,765 --> 00:01:41,463
Think of a processor as a team of workers.

20
00:01:41,463 --> 00:01:48,366
A CPU, the main processor in your laptop, has a few very clever workers who take hard jobs one after another.

21
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:54,879
A GPU, a graphics processing unit, has thousands of simpler workers who all work at the same time.

22
00:01:59,467 --> 00:02:04,976
Graphics needed that, because every pixel on the screen has to be worked out at once.

23
00:02:04,976 --> 00:02:07,646
And here's the key: AI needs it too.

24
00:02:09,667 --> 00:02:15,948
A neural network learns by multiplying huge grids of numbers, called matrices, billions of times over.

25
00:02:15,948 --> 00:02:19,912
Each sum is easy; there are just a mind-boggling number of them.

26
00:02:19,912 --> 00:02:23,510
That's work for thousands of hands, not a few clever ones.

27
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:26,250
Engineers call this parallel computing.

28
00:02:29,933 --> 00:02:31,479
AI uses GPUs in two ways.

29
00:02:31,479 --> 00:02:37,902
Training is when a model learns from mountains of data, which can take thousands of GPUs running for weeks.

30
00:02:37,902 --> 00:02:43,554
Inference is when the trained model answers you, every time you ask a chatbot a question.

31
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:54,259
And GPUs now go well beyond games and chatbots: they also run weather and climate models and help scientists simulate new medicines.

32
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:02,211
But how did a gaming-chip company end up ruling AI?

33
00:03:02,211 --> 00:03:08,266
In 2006, Nvidia launched CUDA, software that let anyone use GPUs for any kind of maths.

34
00:03:08,266 --> 00:03:12,144
Huang told Rogan that at first, "Nobody wanted it."

35
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:17,593
Then came the twist.

36
00:03:17,593 --> 00:03:24,411
In 2012, researchers trained a breakthrough image-recognition network, AlexNet, on two Nvidia gaming cards.

37
00:03:24,411 --> 00:03:29,525
Deep learning took off, and researchers were already writing their code in CUDA.

38
00:03:29,525 --> 00:03:35,634
That's Nvidia's real moat: not just the chip, but the software millions of developers know.

39
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:45,147
In 2016, Huang personally delivered one of his first AI supercomputers to a small lab in San Francisco.

40
00:03:45,147 --> 00:03:46,570
That lab was OpenAI.

41
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:53,074
After ChatGPT arrived on the thirtieth of November 2022, the whole world wanted Nvidia's chips.

42
00:03:53,074 --> 00:04:00,896
And on the twenty-ninth of October 2025, Nvidia became the first company ever valued at five trillion dollars.

43
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,256
Now here's the part most people miss.

44
00:04:04,256 --> 00:04:07,417
A GPU is only as fast as the data you can feed it.

45
00:04:07,417 --> 00:04:12,066
Thousands of workers are useless if they're waiting for numbers to arrive.

46
00:04:12,066 --> 00:04:14,659
Engineers call this the memory wall.

47
00:04:20,367 --> 00:04:22,873
Computers use two main kinds of memory.

48
00:04:22,873 --> 00:04:27,635
DRAM is fast working memory; it forgets everything when the power goes off.

49
00:04:27,635 --> 00:04:32,195
NAND flash is storage, like your phone's; slower, but it remembers.

50
00:04:34,133 --> 00:04:39,074
For AI, the answer is a special kind of DRAM called HBM, high bandwidth memory.

51
00:04:39,074 --> 00:04:42,841
Picture a tower: memory chips stacked one on top of another,

52
00:04:42,841 --> 00:04:49,325
joined by thousands of tiny vertical wires running through the silicon, and placed right beside the GPU.

53
00:04:49,325 --> 00:04:52,898
Short distance, wide road, far more data per second.

54
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:04,304
Only three companies make HBM at scale: SK Hynix and Samsung of South Korea, and Micron of the United States.

55
00:05:04,304 --> 00:05:07,093
Remember it as Korea twice, America once.

56
00:05:07,093 --> 00:05:09,749
SK Hynix is a major supplier to Nvidia,

57
00:05:09,749 --> 00:05:14,530
and demand is so strong that in July 2026 its chief executive said cust

58
00:05:14,530 --> 00:05:19,678
omer demand would stay higher than its supply capacity even beyond 2030.

59
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,943
So one AI chip is really a world project.

60
00:05:25,943 --> 00:05:28,044
Nvidia designs it in America.

61
00:05:28,044 --> 00:05:30,286
TSMC manufactures it in Taiwan.

62
00:05:30,286 --> 00:05:34,580
The memory comes from South Korea and the United States.

63
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:40,483
And India now has a link in this chain: on the twenty-eighth of February 2026,

64
00:05:40,483 --> 00:05:48,821
the Prime Minister inaugurated Micron's plant at Sanand in Gujarat, the first project approved under the India Semiconductor Mission.

65
00:05:48,821 --> 00:05:51,124
It assembles and tests memory chips.

66
00:05:51,124 --> 00:05:56,651
Every link in this chain is strategic, and that's exactly why spies are interested.

67
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,231
So, the allegation.

68
00:05:59,231 --> 00:06:07,474
A honey trap is an old intelligence tactic: using a romantic relationship to gain access to, or leverage over, someone powerful.

69
00:06:09,767 --> 00:06:15,170
Here's the timeline, from reports carried in Indian papers, which cite The New York Times.

70
00:06:15,170 --> 00:06:20,691
In June 2024, Huang was photographed in a Taipei bar with Alice Huang, also called Huang Le,

71
00:06:20,691 --> 00:06:28,529
a Chinese citizen and an executive at Megaspeed, a Singapore-based company that bought Nvidia chips and rented out computing power.

72
00:06:28,529 --> 00:06:30,313
The two are not related.

73
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:44,509
Later in 2024, US officials grew suspicious of a Megaspeed data centre in Malaysia, where Nvidia equipment was still in its boxes.

74
00:06:44,509 --> 00:06:53,474
On the twenty-ninth of October 2026, The New York Times reported that US officials had concluded she was a Chinese intelligence asset.

75
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,635
Now, where each side stands.

76
00:06:57,635 --> 00:07:01,187
Nvidia first said the meetings were ordinary customer contact.

77
00:07:01,187 --> 00:07:04,288
Then, on the eighth of November, Huang wrote to staff,

78
00:07:04,288 --> 00:07:12,200
"I was unfaithful to my wife," and the board said it has "no reason to believe" national security or company secrets were compromised.

79
00:07:16,333 --> 00:07:20,180
China's Foreign Ministry calls the spy claims groundless slander.

80
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:22,277
Megaspeed says it is investigating.

81
00:07:22,277 --> 00:07:26,823
Singapore says its officials did not investigate her over intelligence links.

82
00:07:26,823 --> 00:07:31,552
She has not responded to requests for comment, and no one has been charged.

83
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:40,985
So does this slow down the AI buildout, the race to build giant data centres?

84
00:07:40,985 --> 00:07:43,540
Here's what we know, on both sides.

85
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:47,580
The pressure first.

86
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:48,769
Nvidia's shares slid,

87
00:07:48,769 --> 00:07:55,413
and at Monday's low in Frankfurt trading the company stood to lose about two hundred and seventy billion dollars in value.

88
00:07:55,413 --> 00:07:59,249
One analyst linked the selloff to uncertainty about data-centre plans,

89
00:07:59,249 --> 00:08:04,951
and others warned that a long internal review could delay investment decisions or product launches.

90
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:14,971
Three House committee chairs have called Huang to a closed-door meeting on Nvidia's security against foreign espionage.

91
00:08:14,971 --> 00:08:22,554
And the scandal adds pressure to tighten export rules, including the loophole where Chinese firms rent chips in data centres abroad.

92
00:08:26,867 --> 00:08:28,056
Now the cushion.

93
00:08:28,056 --> 00:08:31,486
Analysts say Nvidia's chips are hard to replace.

94
00:08:31,486 --> 00:08:37,434
South Korea's government said it expects no disruption to contracted GPU deliveries.

95
00:08:37,434 --> 00:08:42,073
And on Monday, the shares of TSMC, Samsung and SK Hynix rose.

96
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:48,503
The real question is not whether AI needs these chips.

97
00:08:48,503 --> 00:08:51,180
It's who gets them, and on whose rules.

98
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:56,295
So why should India care?

99
00:08:56,295 --> 00:08:57,330
Three reasons.

100
00:08:57,330 --> 00:08:58,296
One: compute.

101
00:08:58,296 --> 00:09:02,438
The IndiaAI Mission, approved on the seventh of March 2024,

102
00:09:02,438 --> 00:09:09,893
had built shared computing of more than forty-five thousand GPUs by June 2026, according to the government.

103
00:09:09,893 --> 00:09:14,335
Any change in export rules affects who can get those chips.

104
00:09:18,933 --> 00:09:20,368
Two: manufacturing.

105
00:09:20,368 --> 00:09:28,473
Micron's Sanand plant puts India inside the memory supply chain, and the India Semiconductor Mission wants more.

106
00:09:28,473 --> 00:09:29,979
And three: security.

107
00:09:29,979 --> 00:09:37,524
India's Official Secrets Act, 1923, applies to any person who passes on secrets, not just officials.

108
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:47,176
Here's your memory tip for any espionage question: MICE.

109
00:09:47,176 --> 00:09:49,548
Money, Ideology, Compromise, Ego.

110
00:09:49,548 --> 00:09:53,524
These are the four classic levers used to turn a target.

111
00:09:53,524 --> 00:09:57,033
A honey trap works through the C: compromise.

112
00:10:03,933 --> 00:10:04,610
Exam desk.

113
00:10:04,610 --> 00:10:12,424
Statement one: High Bandwidth Memory is made by stacking memory chips vertically and placing them right next to the processor.

114
00:10:12,424 --> 00:10:12,794
True.

115
00:10:12,794 --> 00:10:15,370
That's how it beats the memory wall.

116
00:10:17,767 --> 00:10:21,235
Statement two: CUDA is a chip made by Nvidia.

117
00:10:21,235 --> 00:10:21,763
False.

118
00:10:21,763 --> 00:10:25,532
CUDA is a software platform; that's the trap.

119
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:34,665
Statement three: India's Official Secrets Act, 1923, applies only to government servants.

120
00:10:34,665 --> 00:10:35,223
False.

121
00:10:35,223 --> 00:10:37,593
It applies to any person.

122
00:10:39,767 --> 00:10:40,695
Now for Mains.

123
00:10:40,695 --> 00:10:47,382
"Semiconductors have become instruments of geopolitics." Examine this with reference to AI chips and India.

124
00:10:47,382 --> 00:10:50,477
Frame it in three parts: why chips are strategic,

125
00:10:50,477 --> 00:10:56,473
how export controls and espionage risks follow, and how India is building its own capacity.

126
00:10:58,967 --> 00:11:00,471
Three things to watch.

127
00:11:00,471 --> 00:11:04,396
One: Huang's closed-door meeting with the House committees.

128
00:11:04,396 --> 00:11:07,732
Two: the findings of Nvidia's board investigation.

129
00:11:07,732 --> 00:11:13,554
And three: whether America tightens its rules on chip sales and remote access for China.

130
00:11:13,554 --> 00:11:18,236
As of the ninth of November 2026, the allegations remain unproven.

131
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:22,595
Next briefing: semiconductors and space diplomacy.

132
00:11:22,595 --> 00:11:26,653
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

